              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fig. 13.11: Graph of the linear equation y = –5x + 3"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.586,
            0.882,
            0.626
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Let us further explore the geometrical meaning of the slope of a line y = mx + d. To do this we will consider the following cases:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.137,
            0.63,
            0.884,
            0.707
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "1. Positive Slope (m > 0). The line goes upward from left to right. As x increases, y increases. In y = 2x + 1, the slope is m = 2. It means for every unit you move to the right, you also go up 2 units."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.137,
            0.712,
            0.884,
            0.79
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "2. Negative Slope (m < 0). The line goes downward from left to right. As x increases, y decreases. In y = –3x + 4, the slope is m = –3. It means for every unit you move to the right, you go down by 3 units."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.137,
            0.795,
            0.884,
            0.854
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3. Zero Slope (m = 0). In this case the line is horizontal and parallel to the x-axis. That is, y remains constant as x changes. For example, in y = 5, the slope = m = 0."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
